WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:07.270
Adam Driver's ascent to the A-list over the
past decade has been as surprising as it has

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:07.980
been fascinating.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.799
The actors' awkwardly rugged good looks certainly
set him apart from the pack, but it's his

00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:16.590
fierce commitment to his craft that garnered
attention from some of the biggest directors

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:17.200
in Hollywood.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.070
It was Driver's work on Lena Dunham's HBO
hit Girls that helped get the attention of

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:27.080
J.J. Abrams, who was looking to cast a relative
unknown as the villain in his new movie.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.870
That movie was none other Star Wars: The Force
Awakens, and the role was the new trilogy's

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:38.130
central bad guy, Kylo Ren. Abrams decided
that Driver would be his Kylo, and he flew

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.620
him out to Bad Robot headquarters and straight
up offered him the part.

00:00:42.109 --> 00:00:46.520
Wary of boarding such a big project, Driver
almost passed on the choice role.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.050
Luckily he took the part and became the dynamic,
complex villain the next generation of Stars

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.160
Wars fans deserved.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.740
Alan Rickman — Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's
Stone

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.400
By now, we're all familiar with the rigorous
casting search that the producers of the Harry

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.820
Potter film franchise underwent to find just
the right youngsters to portray the young

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.900
wizards Harry, Hermione, and Ron.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.200
That search saw producers sorting through
thousands of young British faces and organizing

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:16.330
dozens of auditions before settling on Daniel
Radcliffe, Emma Watson, and Rupert Grint for

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:17.320
the coveted roles.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.720
And even then, those faces had to be approved
by author J.K.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.620
Rowling herself.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.090
Overall, Rowling had plenty of sway over who
appeared in the adaptations of her bestselling

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.000
book series.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:31.820
When it came down to casting the pivotal role
of Severus Snape, Rowling insisted that the

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.060
great Alan Rickman was the only actor who
could play the part right.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.860
"How did that film role come about for you?"

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:40.600
"Um…

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.360
I was asked to do it."

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.020
If you've seen the films, well, it's tough
to argue with her choice.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.920
It's impossible to imagine another actor as
Snape, and the franchise is all the more accessible

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.440
for Rickman's deeply nuanced performance.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.520
Chris Evans — Captain America: The First
Avenger

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.900
There's no denying that Marvel Entertainment
has produced some of the biggest and boldest

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.820
superhero films Hollywood has ever seen.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.080
They've accomplished that feat by thinking
outside the blockbuster box when choosing

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.140
directors to guide their properties through
production a habit that started when they

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.780
tapped Jon Favreau to helm Iron Man.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.320
Still, Marvel doesn't always get enough credit
for making inspired choices in their casting.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.340
Lest we forget, they opened the MCU by hedging
bets on Robert Downey Jr. as Iron Man.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:30.480
Marvel made Downey jump or, rather, fly through
hoops to prove he was right for the job, but

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.090
Chris Evans had a much easier time landing
the lead in Captain America: The First Avenger.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.900
In fact, he didn't even need to audition to
score the coveted role.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.540
Turns out Evans' absurdly all-American looks
had already caught the eye of director Joe

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:48.180
Johnston, who convinced Marvel to cast the
actor without so much as a screen test.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.680
While Evans has become one of the most recognizable
stars in the world since landing the role,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.900
his casting felt like a metered risk at the
time, one we're grateful to Marvel for taking.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.220
Chadwick Boseman — Captain America: Civil
War

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.580
Prior to joining the MCU as the future king
of Wakanda, Chadwick Boseman was already well

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.100
on his way to becoming a star.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.640
In the years leading up to his MCU debut,
he steadily worked his way from bit parts

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.200
on TV shows like Fringe…

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.900
"You realize just what you did to me?

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:23.160
If you had given me some special power, that'd
be one thing — maybe I'd be special."

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.020
...to starring in a couple of the better biopics
produced in the past decade.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.300
"Don't let them get to you."

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:31.520
"I won't.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.320
God built me to last."

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.540
Those choice roles aside, it still seemed
like not enough people knew who Boseman was.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.070
Turns out that while not many audiences knew
Boseman's work, the right people did including

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.420
Captain America: Civil War directors Joe and
Anthony Russo.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:54.060
When Marvel Studios decided it was finally
time to bring Black Panther into the mix,

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:58.050
they almost instantly and unanimously decided
he was not just right for the part of king

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.600
T'Challa, but the only actor who was.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.480
They had Boseman on the phone the next day,
offering him the role without an audition.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.520
"Wakanda forever!"

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.920
Bill Murray — Lost In Translation

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.710
By the time Bill Murray agreed to star in
Sofia Coppola's sophomore film, he already

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:22.090
had a dozen or so unforgettable roles to his
credit.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:26.960
Throughout the '80s and '90s, the legendary
funnyman was delivering iconic work for practically

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.480
every film he appeared in.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:29.640
"I'm a god."

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:30.440
"You're God?"

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.620
"...I'm A god I'm not THE god…

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:34.940
I don't think."

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.120
Seems Coppola was paying attention, and she
was adamant about casting the actor as the

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.040
lead in her existential drama Lost in Translation.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.360
She even wrote the screenplay specifically
with Murray in mind, and claims she wouldn't

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.000
have made the film if he'd turned down the
role, so there was no audition process to

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:50.620
be had.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:57.420
"For a relaxing time, make it Suntory time."

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.300
It actually seems more like Coppola was the
one who auditioned for Murray.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:06.380
As the story goes, Coppola had a mutual friend
pass an early draft of the Lost in Translation

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.200
script along to Murray, who then set up a
meeting.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:13.900
The pair hit it off, and Murray agreed to
do the film only he never actually signed

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.300
a contract to that effect.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.900
On Murray's word alone, Coppola took her cast
and crew to Tokyo and started production,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.740
unsure of whether he'd even show up.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.320
Luckily for her and movie lovers in general
Murray showed.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.980
He also delivered an Oscar-nominated performance
that stands among the finest of his career.

